anslator":[],"publishedOn":"2021-11-30","sourceUrl":"https://globalvoices.org/2021/11/30/a-century-of-etel-adnan-a-mountain-who-leaves-behind-mountains/","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","contentSha256":"6ccd037830511ab74fc38e52ae7796472bdac0e74fccae38cbdaf7e916c034a0","textSha256":"fd0152ffebe21b42764e96652c173fbae57693628b2abe828030aea98523813a","archivedRecordSha256":"5445afeba95d93450c9f99c8cba01ad42fafbe6dec867f1e4bc02d226e732f52","inStudy":"urn:doxa:study:muslim-coverage-1"},{"@id":"urn:doxa:unit:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad:u_7dd421f5c1e2feb1c810c99f","@type":["doxa:SourceUnit","prov:Entity"],"recordId":"u_7dd421f5c1e2feb1c810c99f","status":"accepted","fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad"},{"@id":"urn:doxa:unit:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad:u_9ac0fc75f20d46da5159e4a0","@type":["doxa:SourceUnit","prov:Entity"],"recordId":"u_9ac0fc75f20d46da5159e4a0","status":"accepted","fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad"},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:c6c9a76649a75270ba33c926da752b2928ec391cb3200402e53c5b514ca70bbb","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00013","start":0,"end":164,"exact":"Though she described loving drawing at school, it wasn’t until her mid-thirties that Etel Adnan began to paint; the visual arts at once connected with her writing. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:44fc043c67068f6ecd7fa02d503bda50f817003579c548d0d8ea6292d4b158cf","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00013","start":164,"end":312,"exact":"She saw writing as drawing and drawing as writing, the essence of both a form of photography of the soul, created for others in an ongoing dialogue."},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:61f18111348d5ae0f8fe864b970792b5f9f8c577892967eaa780f8b894a57160","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00014","start":0,"end":81,"exact":"Adnan felt most at home in the terrain of an urban setting surrounded by nature. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:052e6147130b4ba814e5cf5c40d837ee4c82d594ed85e4115f4abbf8dea245a7","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00014","start":81,"end":334,"exact":"She described finding beauty in the urban natural environment of San Francisco, where she would visit Redwood Forest of the Muir Woods, Yosemite National Park and her mountain, Mount Tamalpais, which became a central source of inspiration for her work. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:0e277c522257844690481fa1cb007aca277add89b31d2e2da8d02a273d4dac4a","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00014","start":334,"end":400,"exact":"It reminded her of Beirut, a capital city close to the mountains. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:1f8b0ebcb180dfeb6c06ae000573ca35094ff7ad34fc9964e78ef58433fa3c95","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00014","start":400,"end":544,"exact":"In her writings, drawings, and paintings, mountains were sensual beings, regarded in confluence with the skies and earth, erupting in volcanoes."},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:00119bce06d3d29fb754ed252f9594b2590b8755538c63205746de652b1f3009","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00015","start":0,"end":100,"exact":"Mount Tamalpais recurred often in her paintings, each time in different colours, shapes, and forms. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:34057b1023649efd004a11b57998ea58342e3e94fc87309b8177df7b400c15f6","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00015","start":100,"end":351,"exact":"Speaking of Tamalpais to Simone Fattal, she said that the process of painting and writing gave her the implicit certitude of what the mountain was, of what she could see, but ascribed an autonomy of being to both the mountain and her depictions of it."},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:978f371e87820dea8ae06474fe4d4683a2897b4cf7708c32988149eda9001382","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00016","start":0,"end":145,"exact":"She employed a bright, rich palette that she attributed to her identity as a ‘Californian artist,’ finding joy in constructing things in colour. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:f91f5708fa720c6bbe2a60b58e77577ba0e0677594a07bad66ce0f4f7b20b151","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00016","start":145,"end":190,"exact":"As a child, Adnan wanted to be an architect. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:c4a7f515ffacc330ad2a3cc3b28c2b3c5a764d0752bba228de439a74d95d2241","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00016","start":190,"end":367,"exact":"Upon closer inspection, her paintings reveal what Simone Fattal called the “vocabulary of an architect,” which can be seen in her approach to carefully constructed compositions."},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:e5861f342b835289707c8450fd1efa22e90f2416618e459b6e608b2e478a9a49","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00017","start":0,"end":168,"exact":"Through paintings, drawings, tapestries and leporellos (small booklets folded in concertina), Adnan wove stories and poems in her signature abstract language of light. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:58af2f4943a67c59cc4a974a1f45567cf04a389ea333cdfde22011b8c1b6fcfa","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00017","start":168,"end":479,"exact":"In an interview with Apollo magazine, she describes evoking an internal landscape: “a landscape that is in me.” Exhibitions of her work have been mounted in institutions all over the world, including the SF MoMA; Zentrum Paul Klee, Bern; Institute de Monde Arabe, Paris; and The Serpentine Galleries in London. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:1ac49b1511888c6d0b2a95c78ae8136cc84687606e71861e007f3b3d9c544310","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00017","start":479,"end":597,"exact":"She has exhibited at Documenta 13 in Kassel, Germany; the Sharjah Biennial, UAE; and the Whitney Biennial in New York."},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:458028c3c96f6625339d31ab2b44b1006ecc1100855ac39e659699c3846838bb","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00018","start":0,"end":52,"exact":"Adnan’s work is currently on view at the Solomon R. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:374536dc1584610ab2e477b14963305279488e7c4f16cb3ee61a14b0f6addfd0","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00018","start":52,"end":302,"exact":"Guggenheim Museum in New York, in the exhibition ‘Etel Adnan: Light’s New Measure’ and at Sfeir-Semler Gallery in Hamburg, in an exhibition of new works on the occasion of the 2021 Lichtwark Prize to be awarded to Adnan posthumously on November 18th."},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:b587a3d0753b63bf2521bc6d9b91b33e2b14b5e6b045dd6d0ce2d334ac23457a","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00019","start":0,"end":177,"exact":"In a sense of commitment and responsibility to the world, Adnan gave generously of her time, her gifts and herself, with an increasing sense of urgency towards her final years. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:5103b086f0b4fae4301df716efff44ec3bf578b7f37efc0d4b34990f04a2ead6","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00019","start":177,"end":264,"exact":"She wrote of the universe, of life, death, nature and cities, and mountains and women. "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:a6c520370ba640d86670d93213ff56c27a8c8201ca593f78541be1a8c18c6ebd","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00019","start":264,"end":351,"exact":"In the mornings after her death, the universe mourns its self-proclaimed “best friend.”"},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:9f652cf9f9de0fa685bab74552508e07680e166720307419dc24ec315d7f9d26","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00020","start":0,"end":156,"exact":"Adnan believed that memory works against death, the collective memory acting in what she described as a mad form of denial in disguise (la folie déguisée). "},{"@id":"urn:doxa:passage:cf61bc9c92d202cf91bab41487a8a1bb9854fa34ce49575d381ca11590ff9f11","@type":["doxa:Passage","prov:Entity"],"fromSource":"urn:doxa:source:11060d21b8953b263b2bafa1be781bad","blockId":"gv-751324-b00020","start":156,"end":387,"exact":"In her interview with Ricardo Karam, when he asked her how she wanted to be remembered, she exclaimed that she wanted people “to remember that I love the world, that I loved the universe and being alive was a gift of the universe.”"},{"@id":"urn:doxa:decision:3ea5095a075d35db04c62bcb6d3a9f26","@type":["doxa:Decision","prov:Entity"],"recordId":"3ea5095a075d35db04c62bcb6d3a9f26","outcome":"not_applicable","generatedBy":"urn:doxa:run:b2a2edd4ca7047128a5e433d8a874530","evaluates":"urn:doxa:criterion:anti-muslim-hostility:discrimination_claim:2026-03-operational-1:794fad83018ea1e0de86fe60ff7dec7a18160eddcba979e1750fdd782a8456f3","sco